  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Patchy fog this morning will temporarily reduce\nvisibilities before clearing out after daybreak. High pressure\nbuilds in from the north in the wake of cold front that moved\nthrough last night. Temperatures remain warm Friday afternoon\nwith highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s, but dew points will fall\nslightly, making it feel more comfortable. Cooler temperatures\narrive during the day Saturday with highs only warming up into\nthe 70s. Conditions should start out mostly sunny Saturday \nmorning, then mid and high clouds will build into the region \nthroughout the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A series of cold fronts and low pressure systems\nwill bring unsettled weather to the area starting Saturday night\nand continuing through at least Tuesday. Heavy rain from time \nto time is possible.\n\nHigh pressure moves out to sea Saturday night, and then low \npressure organizes and develops over the Midwest. A warm front \nextending out from that low will lift north through the \nNortheast Saturday night through Sunday morning. Showers first \ndevelop after midnight Saturday night and continue through \nSunday morning.\n\nAs low pressure draws nearer to the region, southerly flow increases \nthroughout the Northeast and low level moisture increases as well \nwith surface dew points rising well into the 60s and low 70s and \nPWATs will be in excess of 2 inches. Low pressure then passes \nthrough the southern Poconos and Lehigh Valley Sunday afternoon and \nevening, resulting in more widespread showers with isolated \nthunderstorms across most of the area during that time. Locally \nheavy rain may develop from time to time. A cold front passes \nthrough the region behind the departing low, and that front will sag \nsouth towards Delmarva by Monday morning, where it will stall\nout. \n\nWPC now has a Slight Risk for Excessive Rainfall (2 out of 4)\nfor Sunday and Sunday night for areas along the I-95 corridor \nand points north and west. A Marginal Risk remains for the rest \nof the region south and east of the I-95 corridor. A Marginal\nRisk also continues for the entire region into Monday.\n\nSeveral more disturbances will pass through the region Monday and \nTuesday, and this will keep rain chances in the forecast during this \ntime. For now, PoPs are limited to 30 to 50 percent (chance)\ngiven the uncertainty of the timing and coverage of showers.",
